1
00:00:00,857 --> 00:00:02,887
A cyber-fraud complaint comes in.

2
00:00:03,137 --> 00:00:06,875
The money has already moved, through wallets nobody can name.

3
00:00:06,922 --> 00:00:11,952
The investigator has an address, and one question: which exchange do I write to?

4
00:00:11,996 --> 00:00:15,490
NOIR answers it. It follows each wallet both ways.

5
00:00:15,544 --> 00:00:17,524
Back, to the exchange that funded it.

6
00:00:17,884 --> 00:00:21,314
Forward, to the exchange account that received its money.

7
00:00:21,357 --> 00:00:25,037
This is a real wallet, from a recorded read of the TRON chain.

8
00:00:25,307 --> 00:00:27,520
MEXC funded one of its payers.

9
00:00:27,561 --> 00:00:31,579
Its money passed through one hop and reached Binance's own wallet.

10
00:00:31,619 --> 00:00:35,275
And part of it reached an address on the OFAC sanctions list.

11
00:00:35,318 --> 00:00:40,856
Attribution is a deterministic lookup, not a guess. No language model decides it.

12
00:00:40,903 --> 00:00:46,043
NOIR can name seventeen exchanges across TRON, Ethereum and Polygon,

13
00:00:46,233 --> 00:00:50,532
and screens every wallet against more than a thousand sanctioned addresses.

14
00:00:50,580 --> 00:00:55,745
Filing is one step. Paste one address, or five hundred, from one case or many.

15
00:00:55,792 --> 00:00:59,085
Each line is checked before a single chain is read.

16
00:01:00,195 --> 00:01:04,623
Every wallet lands on one shared desk, under the exchange it leads to.

17
00:01:04,675 --> 00:01:09,649
At the top, NOIR names the exchange to write to next, and what needs following up.

18
00:01:09,703 --> 00:01:14,432
Wallets that touch a sanctioned address are flagged before any request is sent.

19
00:01:14,478 --> 00:01:19,118
Below is a departures board: each exchange, the wallets waiting on it,

20
00:01:19,218 --> 00:01:23,328
the money that moved, whether it is registered with FIU-IND,

21
00:01:23,368 --> 00:01:25,281
and whether its request has gone.

22
00:01:26,978 --> 00:01:31,048
Open an exchange, and every wallet that leads there is on one page,

23
00:01:31,128 --> 00:01:33,523
from every case, in both directions.

24
00:01:33,575 --> 00:01:37,735
NOIR records how the exchange receives a law-enforcement request,

25
00:01:37,895 --> 00:01:40,500
copied from the exchange's own page.

26
00:01:40,545 --> 00:01:45,423
Each wallet states how much evidence was seen, in words. Never a percentage.

27
00:01:45,468 --> 00:01:49,858
Then one click drafts one consolidated request: KYC, access logs,

28
00:01:49,878 --> 00:01:55,708
transaction history, preservation, and a freeze where the account is known.

29
00:01:56,494 --> 00:02:01,746
It prints as a letter. The legal basis is left blank, for the officer to supply.

30
00:02:03,446 --> 00:02:08,176
NOIR records what each exchange did with it: acknowledged, sent the data,

31
00:02:08,236 --> 00:02:13,949
froze the account, refused, or did not answer. And it warns when a reply is late.

32
00:02:15,902 --> 00:02:20,752
Every wallet keeps its own file: the trace, the route, the patterns observed,

33
00:02:20,882 --> 00:02:23,970
and a hash of every chain response it was read from.

34
00:02:24,024 --> 00:02:27,063
So an attribution can be checked again, by anyone.

35
00:02:28,468 --> 00:02:30,618
NOIR also says what it cannot do.

36
00:02:30,898 --> 00:02:35,289
Its method page lists what is built, what is partial, and what is not.

37
00:02:35,330 --> 00:02:38,450
An unreadable wallet is never reported as empty.

38
00:02:38,780 --> 00:02:43,529
And confidence is how much evidence was seen, never a chance of being right.

39
00:02:44,965 --> 00:02:48,181
NOIR. Every unknown wallet has a destination.
